{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Pow","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.bondSum_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.bondSum_pos","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L172-L180","doc":"The bond sum is **positive and unbounded** once every bond energy is, which\nis the measured case (`0.4899`, `0.6768`, `0.9073`). Together with\n`bondSum_le_linear` this says the additive law grows like `A` and not faster. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.additive_is_linear_multiplicative_is_not","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.additive_is_linear_multiplicative_is_not","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L151-L170","doc":"**The two observables sit in different growth classes.**\n\nWith `A = p + q + 2` nucleons the additive observable is pinned between two lines\nthrough the origin, for *every* `(p, q)` — that is what \"extensive\" means. The\nmultiplicative one is bounded by no line at all, at any scale.\n\nThis is §1.2 and §2 of the decision frame, with no nuclear data in it. The two\nsides are not competing fits of the same shape; they are different shapes, and\nonly one of them can be a binding energy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.linear_le_bondSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.linear_le_bondSum","kind":"theorem","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L140-L149","doc":"The bond sum is bounded **below** by a line through the origin in `A−1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.bondSum_le_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.bondSum_le_linear","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L129-L138","doc":"The bond sum is bounded **above** by a line through the origin in `A−1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.bondSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.bondSum","kind":"def","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L123-L127","doc":"The bond-energy sum of the canonical `A−1` chain, for `Z = p+1` protons and\n`N = q+1` neutrons: `p` proton-proton bonds, one proton-neutron interface, and\n`q` neutron-neutron bonds. Equals `log(tr_M(β_free) / tr_M(β_atom))` with\n`epp = -ln b_pp`, `epn = -ln b_pn`, `enn = -ln b_nn`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.exists_pow_gt_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.exists_pow_gt_linear","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L80-L116","doc":"**No constant scale makes an exponential linear.**\n\nFor any base `t > 1`, any scale `s > 0` and any linear coefficient `C`, there is\nan `n` with `C·n < s·t^n`.\n\nThis is the refutation of `B = s·Δtr` in its entirety: `Δtr` carries `t_p^Z` with\n`t_p > 1` (measured 1.1458), binding energy is `Θ(A)`, and no choice of `s`\ncloses that gap. Measured counterpart: at the law's own optimal scale the\nresidual still spans 4.21 decades over the AME2020 table. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.sq_mul_sub_one_le_pow_two_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingExtensivity.sq_mul_sub_one_le_pow_two_mul","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingExtensivity.lean#L63-L78","doc":"Bernoulli, squared: for `1 ≤ t` and any `m`, `t^(2m) ≥ (m(t-1))²`.\n\nOne application of `one_add_mul_le_pow` gives `1 + m(t-1) ≤ t^m`; squaring turns\nthe linear lower bound into a quadratic one, which is what outruns `C·n`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}